.TH QUOTA-SET 8 "October 2026" "SASD Freeware" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
quota-set \- preview or apply complete Linux disk quota limits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B quota-set
.RB ( --user " NAME | " --group " NAME )"
.B --filesystem
.I MOUNTPOINT
.B --block-soft
.I N
.B --block-hard
.I N
.B --inode-soft
.I N
.B --inode-hard
.I N
.RB [ --dry-run " | " --apply " " --confirm-apply " [" --allow-unlimited "]]"
.br
.B quota-set
.RB [ --help " | " --version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Previews a complete user/group quota record by default. Only
.B --apply --confirm-apply
runs Linux
.BR setquota (8).
All four limits are mandatory. Block limits are in 1024-byte units; inode limits count files.
Zero means unlimited. To apply any zero limit, pass
.BR --allow-unlimited .
A positive soft limit may not exceed a positive hard limit. Missing fields are not inferred from existing quotas.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --user NAME
Select a user resolvable through the system name service.
.TP
.B --group NAME
Select a group instead of a user.
.TP
.B --filesystem MOUNTPOINT
Absolute mountpoint directory; exact entry in /proc/self/mounts required for changes.
.TP
.B --block-soft N
Soft limit in KiB.
.TP
.B --block-hard N
Hard limit in KiB.
.TP
.B --inode-soft N
Soft file count.
.TP
.B --inode-hard N
Hard file count.
.TP
.B --dry-run
Show the proposed operation without changes (default).
.TP
.B --apply
Request execution of setquota. Requires --confirm-apply.
.TP
.B --confirm-apply
Mandatory separate approval for --apply.
.TP
.B --allow-unlimited
Acknowledge zero values meaning unlimited.
.TP
.BR --help , " --version"
Show usage or version.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
quota-set --user alice --filesystem /home \\
  --block-soft 100000 --block-hard 120000 \\
  --inode-soft 20000 --inode-hard 25000
.fi
.SH SECURITY
An effective-root invocation rejects backend paths that are not root-owned and protected against group/other writing. The tool does not invoke a shell or install required quota utilities. It does not enable quotas, change grace periods, automatically restore earlier settings, or verify the resulting values. Review the current settings before use; run
.BR quota-report (1)
afterwards.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on successful preview or successful backend exit; 2 for invalid input/authorization; 3 for prerequisites, unsupported systems, untrusted backend, or native failures.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR setquota (8),
.BR quota (1),
.BR quota-report (1),
.BR quota-status (1)
